# Theme-Aware UI

Every visible element (text, labels, backgrounds, borders, inputs, buttons) must work in **both** light and dark modes. The app toggles theme via a `dark` class on `html`; Tailwind’s `dark:` variants apply in dark mode.

## Prefer shared classes (src/style.css)

Use these instead of inline theme styles when possible:

- **Containers:** `card` or `card-elevated`
- **Form controls:** `input-field`; labels in a `.form-row` get theme-aware label styles
- **Buttons:** `btn-primary`, `btn-ghost`, `btn-secondary`, `btn-danger`

## When using raw Tailwind, always pair light + dark

Never use only dark-only classes (e.g. `text-white`, `bg-white/10`, `ring-white/20`) without a light-mode base.

| Purpose | Light base | Dark variant |
|--------|------------|--------------|
| Headings / primary text | `text-gray-900` | `dark:text-white` |
| Body / secondary text | `text-gray-600` or `text-gray-700` | `dark:text-white/70` or `dark:text-white/90` |
| Muted / captions | `text-gray-500` | `dark:text-white/60` |
| Backgrounds (cards, panels) | `bg-gray-100` or `bg-gray-200` | `dark:bg-white/5` or `dark:bg-white/10` |
| Rings / borders | `ring-gray-200` / `border-gray-200` | `dark:ring-white/20` / `dark:border-white/20` |
| Placeholders | `placeholder-gray-500` | `dark:placeholder-white/60` |

**Exception:** Leave `text-white` on solid primary/danger buttons (e.g. `bg-primary-600 text-white`, `bg-blue-600 text-white`).

## Examples

```html
<!-- ❌ BAD: dark-only, invisible in light mode -->
<p class="text-white/80">Description</p>
<div class="bg-white/5 ring-1 ring-white/20 p-4">...</div>
<input class="bg-white/10 border border-white/20 text-white" />

<!-- ✅ GOOD: theme-aware -->
<p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-white/80">Description</p>
<div class="bg-gray-100 dark:bg-white/5 ring-1 ring-gray-200 dark:ring-white/20 p-4">...</div>
<input class="input-field" />
```

## Dynamic / JS class strings

When building classes in JS (e.g. status badges, `getStatusClass()`), return strings that include both light and dark variants (e.g. `'bg-gray-200 dark:bg-white/10 text-gray-700 dark:text-white/70'`).
